        <div id="choosing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Choosing and Testing a Shape Before You Buy
          </h2>

          <p>
            The best way to pick a shape is to hit with each one. Many clubs and courts have players happy to let you try their paddle for a few rallies, and some retailers run demo programs. Play the same drills with a standard and an elongated paddle back to back: a few minutes of dinking, some hands battles at the net and some drives from the baseline. Pay attention to where your mishits go and how quickly you can reposition the paddle, not just how hard the ball comes off it.
          </p>

          <p>
            When you read a spec sheet, look at four numbers together: length, width, handle length and weight. Length and width tell you the shape. Handle length tells you whether a two-handed backhand will fit and how much of the paddle's length is face versus grip. Weight, combined with length, gives you a rough sense of swing weight; a heavier paddle that is also long will feel noticeably slower than either property suggests on its own.
          </p>

          <p>
            Do not let shape crowd out the other decisions. Face material, core thickness, grip size and weight affect how a paddle plays at least as much as its outline. A comfortable grip on the wrong shape will still serve you better than the right shape on a grip that hurts your hand. Our <a href="/best-pickleball-paddle/">best pickleball paddles</a> roundup lists the shape of every pick, which makes it easy to compare like with like.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, remember that shape is not permanent. Many players own two paddles for different situations, perhaps a standard paddle for social doubles and an elongated one for singles. Paddles also wear out with heavy play, and our guide to <a href="/how-long-do-pickleball-paddles-last/">how long pickleball paddles last</a> explains the signs. When you replace one, you will know far more about your game than when you bought the first, and that is the right moment to reconsider the shape. If budget is part of the decision, our breakdown of <a href="/how-much-does-a-pickleball-paddle-cost/">what a pickleball paddle costs</a> shows where the price jumps actually buy you something.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying elongated because a pro uses one</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Professional players hit the center of the paddle almost every time and have the hand speed to manage the extra swing weight. Most recreational players do not, and the narrower face punishes the mishits they make. Start from how you play, not from what you watched on a broadcast.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring swing weight</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Two paddles at the same listed weight can feel very different because a longer paddle carries its mass further from your hand. Compare weight and length together, and if you can, try the paddle in fast hands exchanges before buying.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming a bigger sweet spot claim applies across shapes</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                When an elongated paddle advertises a large sweet spot, it is usually large for an elongated paddle. The basic geometry still means a narrower face than a standard shape, so off-center hits toward the edge are less forgiving.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Crediting the shape for what the core is doing</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Power, pop and feel come from core thickness, face material and construction as much as outline. Compare paddles of similar build when judging shape, or you may switch shapes to fix a problem that was really the core.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Forgetting the handle</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A longer face often comes with a shorter handle, and an elongated paddle with a long handle can feel very different from one without. If you use a two-handed backhand, check handle length specifically rather than assuming every elongated paddle has room for both hands.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the size limit for a pickleball paddle?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Under the 2025 USA Pickleball rulebook, the combined length and width of a paddle, including any edge guard and butt cap, cannot exceed 24 inches, and the length alone cannot exceed 17 inches. There is no limit on thickness or weight in the amateur rules. Check the current rulebook on the USA Pickleball site, since equipment rules are updated from time to time.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is an elongated paddle better for beginners?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually not. Beginners benefit most from the wider sideways sweet spot of a standard or widebody paddle, which hides inconsistent contact and is easier to maneuver at the net. Once you know your style and hit the center of the face reliably, an elongated paddle becomes a reasonable option to try.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do elongated paddles have more power?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                On full swings, generally yes, because the longer lever moves the face faster for the same swing at the handle. The difference is smallest on soft shots and fast reactions at the net. Core and face construction also have a large effect on power, so a short paddle with a powerful build can out-hit a soft elongated one.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are elongated paddles good for doubles?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                They can be, especially for aggressive players who drive and attack, but many doubles players prefer a standard or hybrid shape because most doubles points are decided by control and fast hands at the kitchen line. If you want some extra reach without the full penalty in hand speed, a hybrid is the common compromise.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is a hybrid pickleball paddle?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A hybrid paddle sits between standard and elongated, typically about 16.25 to 16.5 inches long and 7.5 to 7.75 inches wide. It adds some reach and leverage over a standard paddle while keeping more face width than a fully elongated model. Exact dimensions vary by brand, so check the spec sheet of any model you are considering.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why does my elongated paddle feel heavy if it weighs the same?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Because of swing weight. A longer paddle carries its mass further from your wrist, so it takes more effort to rotate even when the total weight matches a shorter paddle. That is why elongated paddles can feel slower in hands battles. Many players adapt after a few sessions, but some never prefer it.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a widebody paddle worth considering?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, if forgiveness and fast hands matter more to you than reach. A widebody shape gives the broadest sideways sweet spot and keeps the head close to your hand, which suits beginners, net-focused doubles players and anyone who struggles with off-center hits. The cost is less reach and less leverage on full swings.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

